e agents. Since the maximum metal excretion depends on the chelating agent's half-life, the appropriate urine collection protocol must be followed. \.br\\.br\Urine analysis allows close monitoring of a patient's response to chelation therapy. In addition, urine mineral analysis reflects the body's immediate nutritional status, and factors influencing excretion. However, blood mineral analysis and other mineral assays are better indicators of a patient's nutritional status.\.br\\.br\Elevated results indicate exposure.\.br\Normal results exclude recent, clinically significant exposure to those metals analysed. Higher cut-offs may apply for industrial exposure.\.br\For further details relating to environmental exposure and guidelines, please refer to the following resources:\.br\   Health monitoring - Resources \F\ Safe Work Australia\.br\   RCPA - Heavy metals urine\.br\\.br\||||||F|||20260521140300|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|3|NM|CrSpUr^Creatinine, Spot Urine^0001||11.4|mmol/L|3.5 - 22.9||||F|||20260515154400|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|4|NM|42220-4^Chromium (Cr)^LN^Crus^Chromium, Urine Spot^L||0.14|ug/gCR|< 4.60||||F|||20260520191000|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|5|NM|34270-9^Cobalt (Co)^LN^Cous^Cobalt, Urine Spot^L||0.03|ug/gCR|< 1.60||||F|||20260520191000|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|6|NM|13829-7^Copper (Cu)^LN^Cuus^Copper, Urine Spot^L||5.77|ug/gCR|< 50.00||||F|||20260520191100|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|7|NM|27411-8^Iron (Fe)^LN^Feus^Iron, Urine Spot^L||22.34|ug/gCR|||||F|||20260521140300|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|8|NM|27367-2^Manganese (Mn)^LN^Mnus^Manganese, Urine Spot^L||0.18|ug/gCR|< 1.70||||F|||20260520191000|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|9|NM|73583-7^Molybdenum (Mo)^LN^Mous^Molybdenum, Urine Spot^L||11.43|ug/gCR|9.70 - 100.00||||F|||20260520191100|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|10|NM|13467-6^Selenium (Se)^LN^Seus^Selenium, Urine Spot^L||20.79|ug/gCR|10.00 - 35.00||||F|||20260520191100|0001^NutriPATH^nutrpathOBX|11|NM|13831-3^Vanadium (V)^LN^Vanus^Vanadium, Ur